  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nThe ridge and high pressure over the Southeast break down as a \ndeep trough moves into the Midwest and then into the Ohio Valley \nby afternoon. The surface low will move through the Great Lakes \nand into New England bringing a cold front through the Tennessee \nValley this afternoon. Winds will be gusty this afternoon and \nevening with gusts up to 30 mph possible at times. The best chance\nfor showers will be Southwest Virginia, Northeast Tennessee and \nalong the East Tennessee mountains and foothills this afternoon \nthrough tonight. Elsewhere, showers, if any, will be isolated. \nThunder chances will be low with low instability expected. A \nchangeover to light snow in the late night/early morning hours \nwill be possible on the highest mountain peaks with little to no \naccumulation expected.\n\nThis weekend will be dry and much cooler with highs in the 50s and \nlower 60s Saturday. Saturday will be breezy as well with gusts up to \n25 mph likely at times. Only slightly warmer on Sunday with highs in \nthe mid 60s. Temperatures will be cold enough for frost on Saturday \nand Sunday nights mainly in Southwest Virginia, Northeast Tennessee \nand the higher elevations. \n\nEarly and mid next week will be dry with highs warming into the 70s \nby Tuesday as ridging and high pressure dominate the Southeast.",
